  "executive_briefing": "As of mid-2026, the international tax landscape remains defined by a fragmented approach to global minimum taxation and an intensified era of unilateral economic statecraft via tariffs. While the OECD's Pillar Two framework has transitioned into a 'Fatigue' phase\u2014marked by national carve-outs and a retreat toward domestic tax sovereignty\u2014nations are increasingly prioritizing fiscal independence. Concurrently, we are witnessing a global acceleration in automated, real-time transactional tax compliance, with mandatory e-invoicing becoming the new 'Breakthrough' standard for digital auditing. The escalation of tariff volatility, particularly through renewed U.S. enforcement on key commodities, solidifies 'Peak Hype' as the dominant status for trade policy, as MNEs struggle to navigate shifting border tax environments.",
